  // any behaviour, style or animation may add "when":"Q3 = 2 AND Q1 >= 18" — it then applies only while that holds ("when": null removes it)
  // on: answer · change · select_option (with "options") · deselect_option · page_complete · block_complete · appear · page_enter · click · hover
  // effects: animate {preset} · show_message {text: plain text} · hide_message · show_popup {text, title, placement, dismiss, autoMs} · hide_popup · add_class / remove_class / toggle_class {className} · set_style {style} · show · hide · scroll_into_view · focus — each with an optional "target" (default: the behaviour's own)
  // select_option, page_complete, block_complete and hover HOLD while true and are undone when they stop being true; a style rule with "whenClass":"chosen" applies while add_class "chosen" is on
  // attach_behavior_to_question / _option / _block / _page = create_behavior on that kind of target
CHOOSING THE PATTERN:
• Conditional on an answer ("when Q3 has option 1 selected…") → a declarative behaviour: "on" + "options" (the option CODES in the outline) + "when" for more. select_option holds while selected and undoes itself after — nothing to close or clean up.
• A popup → the show_popup effect, never a hand-made overlay. "placement": "center" (modal) for confirmations, eligibility, anything that must be seen · "anchored" (next to its "target", e.g. "Q3.option:1") for a hint about one element · "top" banner · "inline" in the card; plain feedback beside the answer is show_message. "dismiss": "button" (default) | "auto" (+"autoMs") | "none" (not for center). On a momentary event (answer, click, appear) say how it closes.
• A script only for what effects cannot express — and then rs.popup / rs.closePopup / rs.showMessage, never your own position:fixed element (refused).
• Target "Q3" runs on Q3's page, "survey" on every page; every time unless "once": true.
• One mechanism per behaviour: if the outline lists one for the same target and event, update it by id (a second is refused). A small script change is update_behavior "edits", not the whole script.
Worked example — "When Q3 has option 1 selected, display a popup" (Q3's option 1 in the outline):
{"op":"create_behavior","label":"Q3 option 1 popup","target":"Q3","on":"select_option","options":["1"],"effects":[{"do":"show_popup","title":"Thank you","text":"Thanks — a few more questions about this follow.","placement":"center"}]}
{"op":"create_behavior","label":"…","target":"Q5","script":"rs.listen('select', 'self', (e) => { if (e.option === '99') rs.showMessage('self', 'Tell us more below.'); });"}
  // JAVASCRIPT — the ONE place survey JavaScript lives (never write it anywhere else). Use it when the researcher asks for code or nothing above can do it. It runs in a sandbox with TWO things:
  // (1) the rs api: rs.listen(event, target, fn) — events answer change select deselect click hover page complete; fn gets {question, value, option}
  //     rs.getAnswer("Q3") rs.getQuestion("Q3") rs.getBlock() rs.getPage() rs.addClass(t, name) rs.removeClass(t, name) rs.toggleClass(t, name) rs.animate(t, preset, {duration}) rs.setStyle(t, {prop: value}) rs.clearStyle(t) rs.show(t) rs.hide(t) rs.showMessage(t, text) rs.hideMessage(t) rs.scrollTo(t) rs.focus(t) rs.after(ms, fn) rs.log(…) rs.popup(text, {title, placement, target, dismiss}) rs.closePopup()   — t is a target string above or "self".
  // (2) the survey's document — a real DOM of the page, so document.querySelector / querySelectorAll / closest / classList / dataset / getAttribute / textContent / value / MutationObserver / addEventListener all work, and loops are fine (for…of, while with braces, forEach; one that never ends is stopped).
  //     THE REAL PAGE HOOKS — use these, never guess class names: the Next/Submit button is [data-testid="rs-next"], Back is [data-testid="rs-back"], a question's card is [data-qid="<question id>"] (the ids are in the outline) or [data-rs-el="question"], an option is [data-rs-el="option"], the progress bar is .rs-progress-fill, the button row is .rs-nav, a text or number question's answer box is [data-qid="<question id>"] input (select / textarea for those kinds). The survey's elements carry NO name, data-variable, data-role or data-name attributes and no other tool's classes: `input[name="AGE"]`, `[data-variable=…]`, `.sd-question`, `.next-button`, `#next`, `.question` match NOTHING — the Studio refuses a script whose selector can only match nothing.
  //     Events: click input change focus blur keydown keyup mouseover mouseout mouseenter mouseleave dblclick — handled with addEventListener on any element (or on document). A handler runs once per event.
  //     What a script CAN change: add/remove class names (not the survey's own rs-… ones), set inline styles (el.style.x = …; checked by the CSS gate), set hidden / disabled / title / role / tabindex / aria-* / data-* attributes (never hidden / disabled on Next or Back — the survey keeps them working), and create its OWN elements (div span p b i em strong small br ul ol li a button label h1–h6 code pre blockquote …) with document.createElement + appendChild/insertBefore/remove, whose text and attributes it may then change.
  //     What it CANNOT: change an answer (no .value / .checked =, no el.click() on an option, no dispatchEvent — a starting answer is set_default_value below), cancel an event (preventDefault does nothing: the click has already happened), rewrite text the survey drew (create an element instead, or rs.showMessage), use window / fetch / XMLHttpRequest / localStorage / cookies / setInterval / eval / location / parent / postMessage (setTimeout and rs.after are fine, up to 60 s).
  //     A script runs when its page opens (the elements it names exist by then — never wait for them with a setTimeout that calls the function again; for ones that appear later use a MutationObserver or rs.listen) and is torn down when the page is left — never add a handler a second time to "make sure". Errors and refusals are written to the browser console and the preview inspector.
  // STOPPING THE RESPONDENT (an age limit, an eligibility rule, a required format, "Next stays off until …") is the question's VALIDATION, never a script: set_validation with the rule and its message — the survey then refuses Next, shows the message and the server checks it again. A script can only add the live feedback while the respondent types. A request for "JavaScript that shows a message and disables Next until the value is X" is therefore BOTH:
{"op":"set_validation","target":"Q4","rules":[{"kind":"integer"},{"kind":"min_value","value":10,"message":"Please enter 10 or more."}]}
{"op":"create_behavior","label":"Q4 live feedback","target":"Q4","script":"var box = document.querySelector('[data-qid=\"q4\"] input'); var note = document.createElement('div'); box.parentElement.appendChild(note); function show() { var n = parseInt(box.value, 10); note.textContent = isNaN(n) ? '' : (n >= 10 ? 'Thank you.' : 'Please enter 10 or more.'); } box.addEventListener('input', show); show();"}
